sec.p:
We describe the decline in the concentration of live K562 cells due to NK cell-mediated killing during the 15 h observation time as a first-order enzyme reaction of the form \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$[{\mathrm{NK}}] + [{\mathrm{K}}562_{{\mathrm{live}}}] \to ^k[{\mathrm{NK}}] + [{\mathrm{K}}562_{{\mathrm{dead}}}]$$\end{document}[NK]+[K562live]→k[NK]+[K562dead], whereby the NK cells serve as the enzyme catalyzing this reaction, and k is the reaction rate (the killing efficiency). We assume that k remains constant and that the cytotoxicity of NK cells does not exhaust over time, which is an oversimplification, but justified by the high ratio of NK cells to target cells. Moreover, we consider the average concentration of motile NK cells ([NK]) as being constant throughout the measurement. Finally, we assume that the reverse reaction does not take place, implying that K562 cells do not recover once they are undergoing apoptosis or lysis. Therefore, if a K562 cell forms small temporary blebs that disappear after a while, so that the cell appears alive for the remainder of the experiment, this cell is counted as living.

The dynamics of the above killing reaction can be described with a first-order differential equation, which has the solution1\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$k = \frac{{{\mathrm{ln}}[{\mathrm{K}}562_{{\mathrm{live}},t = 0}] - {\mathrm{ln}}[{\mathrm{K}}562_{{\mathrm{live}},t = T}]}}{{[{\mathrm{NK}}_{{\mathrm{motile}}}] \cdot T}}.$$\end{document}k=ln[K562live,t=0]−ln[K562live,t=T][NKmotile]⋅T.

As individual experiments i have different cell concentrations, we compute weighted mean killing efficiencies2\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$k_{{\mathrm{avg}}} = \frac{{\mathop {\sum}\nolimits_i {k_i} \cdot [{\mathrm{K}}562_{{\mathrm{live}},t = 0}]_i \cdot [{\mathrm{NK}}_{{\mathrm{motile}}}]_i}}{{\mathop {\sum}\nolimits_i {[{\mathrm{K}}562_{{\mathrm{live}},t = 0}]_i} \cdot [{\mathrm{NK}}_{{\mathrm{motile}}}]_i}},$$\end{document}kavg=∑iki⋅[K562live,t=0]i⋅[NKmotile]i∑i[K562live,t=0]i⋅[NKmotile]i,

for both fresh and cryopreserved NK cells. Below, we also report an apparent or composite killing efficiency for all NK cells regardless of their ability to move, which is computed according to3\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$k_{{\mathrm{composite}}} = \frac{{{\mathrm{ln}}[{\mathrm{K}}562_{{\mathrm{live}},t = 0}] - {\mathrm{ln}}[{\mathrm{K}}562_{{\mathrm{live}},t = T}]}}{{([{\mathrm{NK}}_{{\mathrm{motile}}}] + [{\mathrm{NK}}_{{\mathrm{non}} {\mathrm{motile}}}]) \cdot T}}.$$\end{document}kcomposite=ln[K562live,t=0]−ln[K562live,t=T]([NKmotile]+[NKnonmotile])⋅T.
